-- title:
--   Riemann–Roch bound for mod-3 cusp forms of weight 2m
-- statement:
--   Let $N\ge 1$ and suppose $3\nmid N$ and $\nu_3(N)=0$, where $\nu_3(N)=\#\{x\in\mathbf Z/N:\ x^2+x+1=0\}$. Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $3$ and let $m\ge 1$. Write $\bar\jmath=$ `jqModC K` $=q^{-1}\cdot\jmath_{\mathrm{num}}\in K((q))$ for the reduction of the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant, and let $F=$ `modularFunctionFieldFullC K N` be the intermediate field of $K((q))$ over $K$ generated by the series $\bar\jmath(q^{d})$ for the nonzero divisors $d\mid N$. Let $\iota$ be a finite index type and $G:\iota\to F$ a family such that each $G_i$, viewed in $K((q))$, satisfies the weight-$2m$ cuspidal integrality conditions `IsModPCuspFormFn K m`: $G_i^{6}\,\bar\jmath^{\,4m}(\bar\jmath-1728)^{3m}$ is integral over $K[\bar\jmath]$, and for some $M\in\mathbf N$ the element $G_i^{2M}\,\bar\jmath^{\,mM+1}(\bar\jmath-1728)^{mM}$ is integral over $K[\bar\jmath^{-1}]$. Assume the family $G$ is $K$-linearly independent. Then, in $\mathbf Q$, $$\#\iota\ \le\ (2m-1)\bigl(g(N)-1\bigr)+\lfloor m/2\rfloor\,\nu_2(N)+\lfloor 2m/3\rfloor\,\nu_3(N)+(m-1)\,\nu_\infty(N)+[m=1],$$ where $\nu_2(N)=\#\{x\in\mathbf Z/N: x^2+1=0\}$, $\nu_\infty(N)=\sum_{d\mid N}\varphi(\gcd(d,N/d))$, $g(N)=1+\psi(N)/12-\nu_2(N)/4-\nu_3(N)/3-\nu_\infty(N)/2$ with $\psi(N)=\sum_{d\mid N,\ d\text{ squarefree}}N/d$, the floors are natural-number divisions, and $[m=1]$ is $1$ if $m=1$ and $0$ otherwise. Under the hypothesis $\nu_3(N)=0$ the third term vanishes.
--
--   This is the characteristic-$3$ case of the Riemann–Roch upper bound for the dimension of the space of cuspidal mod-$p$ modular forms of level $N$ and weight $2m$, written in terms of the functions $G=f/(\theta\bar\jmath)^m$ in the level-$N$ modular function field; the right-hand side is the classical dimension of $S_{2m}(\Gamma_0(N))$, and at $p=3$ the bound is asserted only for levels with no elliptic points of order $3$. It feeds the comparison of cuspidal mod-$3$ form counts used in the level-lowering input at $p=3$.
-- source:
